  "reasoning_content": "We need answer user: \"Write a detailed tutorial on implementing a bounded LRU cache in Python using collections.OrderedDict. Include a complete class, explain get and put behavior, discuss edge cases, and include tests. Continue with a worked example and complexity analysis. Be precise and use meaningful prose and code.\"\n\nWe need produce final answer with detailed tutorial. Need be precise. We can include code, tests, worked example, complexity. Use OrderedDict. Bounded LRU cache: capacity maybe. Need explain get/put: get returns value and moves key to end (most recently used); put updates existing moves to end, if new and capacity exceeded removes oldest (first item). Edge cases: capacity nonnegative? zero capacity? invalid key/value? get missing returns None? Or optional default. Could implement with __getitem__ etc? Complete class maybe LRUCache. Need tests maybe unittest. Worked example maybe trace. Complexity O(1) average due to OrderedDict operations O(1) amortized? In Python OrderedDict move_to_end O(1), popitem(last=False) O(1). Need discuss OrderedDict insertion order: first is oldest, last most recent.\n\nNeed ensure code correct. Let's design:\n\nfrom collections import OrderedDict\n\nclass LRUCache:\n    def __init__(self, capacity: int) -> None:\n        if capacity < 0: raise ValueError\n        self._capacity = capacity\n        self._store: OrderedDict = OrderedDict()\n\n    def get(self, key):\n        if key not in self._store: return None\n        self._store.move_to_end(key)\n        return self._store[key]\n\n    def put(self, key, value):\n        if key in self._store:\n            self._store[key] = value\n            self._store.move_to_end(key)\n        else:\n            if self._capacity == 0: return\n            self._store[key] = value\n            if len(self._store) > self._capacity:\n                self._store.popitem(last=False)\n\nMaybe get returns None by default; can include default param: get(key, default=None) to handle None values. But classic LeetCode returns None. Edge cases: storing None values; get missing ambiguous if return None. Could use sentinel or default. To be precise, maybe implement get(key, default=MISSING) returns sentinel if missing; but user likely expects None. Could mention limitation. We can support default:\n\n_MISSING = object()",
